# Magic milk: soap sends the colours racing
#
# A child-friendly surface-motion activity with whole milk, food colouring and
# a small amount of dish soap. Food colouring can stain: wear goggles, protect
# clothes and the table, ask an adult before using food, and never taste
# anything from a lab vessel.
register lv1

# Place three small colour drops on still milk. Their resolved dye inventory is
# conserved, but it stays localized at the surface instead of instantly
# tinting the whole beaker.
add v1 whole_milk 100mL
add v1 food_colour_red 0.2mL
add v1 food_colour_yellow 0.2mL
add v1 food_colour_blue 0.2mL
inspect v1

# Now add dish soap without stirring. The recipe-declared surfactant dose
# computes the outward spread, emits SurfaceColourSpread and drives the
# coloured streak animation. The paths are an educational surface observable,
# not a CFD prediction of one exact real-life pattern.
add v1 dish_soap 0.5mL
inspect v1

# Make a fair control with equal milk and equal colour doses, but stir the dye
# into the bulk before adding soap. Stirring emits SurfaceColourMixed and hands
# the same conserved chromophores to the normal Beer-Lambert mixture model.
# With no localized drops left, later soap must not invent a rainbow event.
new beaker
add v2 whole_milk 100mL
add v2 food_colour_red 0.2mL
add v2 food_colour_yellow 0.2mL
add v2 food_colour_blue 0.2mL
stir v2 300rpm 2s
add v2 dish_soap 0.5mL
inspect v2

# Try changing one dye amount or the detergent dose in a fresh vessel. The
# model conserves colour intensity and order, but does not claim molecular milk
# composition, universal surface tension or literal streak trajectories.
